<div class="wm-pop" data-for="model" hidden><button type="button" class="wm-close" aria-label="Close">×</button><strong>World model</strong><p>The brain’s internal model of how things work: causal knowledge about the self and the world, and what is worth pursuing. It is what lets us plan, imagine and learn without having to act first.</p></div><div class="wm-pop" data-for="self" hidden><button type="button" class="wm-close" aria-label="Close">×</button><strong>Causal knowledge about the self</strong><p>Transition priors about oneself: what one can do and what one’s actions bring about — how controllable outcomes are, how one copes, how one is seen by others.</p></div><div class="wm-pop" data-for="world" hidden><button type="button" class="wm-close" aria-label="Close">×</button><strong>Causal knowledge about the world</strong><p>Transition priors about the environment: expectations about how situations unfold — what follows what, how likely harm is, and how long it lasts.</p></div><div class="wm-pop" data-for="goals" hidden><button type="button" class="wm-close" aria-label="Close">×</button><strong>Intrinsic goals</strong><p>What matters at the deepest level — safety, connection, competence, understanding. They set the rewards that every task is evaluated against.</p></div><div class="wm-pop" data-for="tasks" hidden><button type="button" class="wm-close" aria-label="Close">×</button><strong>Task representations</strong><p>The specific model built for the situation at hand. Each inherits its structure from the priors and goals above it, so a bias higher up — overstating threat or understating control, as in anxiety — reshapes every task below.</p></div>
